---
import CalcPage from '../components/CalcPage.astro';
import def from '../calculators/mulch';
import { bulkResult, coverageFt2PerYd3 } from '../lib/bulk';
import { fmt } from '../lib/format';

const ex = bulkResult(20 * 4 * 0.25, 10, 0.3, [2, 3]);
const coverage = [1, 2, 3, 4].map((d) => ({ d, yd: coverageFt2PerYd3(d), bag2: 2 / (d / 12), bag3: 3 / (d / 12) }));
---

<CalcPage
  slug="mulch-calculator"
  def={def}
  title="Mulch Calculator: How Many Yards or Bags of Mulch You Need"
  description="Free mulch calculator. Enter your bed size and depth to get cubic yards of bulk mulch or the number of 2 and 3 cubic foot bags."
  h1="Mulch Calculator"
  updated="2026-09-26"
>
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    Find how many cubic yards of bulk mulch, or how many 2 and 3 cubic foot bags, you need to cover your beds. Add each bed
    to a project to get one total.
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="how-to">How much mulch do I need?</h2>
  <p>
    Multiply the bed's length by its width to get square feet, multiply by the depth in feet, and divide by 27 for cubic
    yards. At the usual 3-inch depth, <strong>one cubic yard of mulch covers 108 square feet</strong>.
  </p>
  <p class="formula">cubic yards = length (ft) × width (ft) × depth (in) ÷ 12 ÷ 27</p>

  <h2 id="example">Example: a 20 × 4 ft bed, 3 inches deep</h2>
  <p>
    20 × 4 = 80 square feet. 80 × 0.25 ft = 20 cubic feet, or 0.74 cubic yards. With 10% extra for settling that is
    {fmt(ex.totalFt3)} cubic feet: {ex.bags[0].count} bags of 2 cubic feet or {ex.bags[1].count} bags of 3 cubic feet.
  </p>

  <h2 id="bags-per-yard">How many bags of mulch are in a yard?</h2>
  <p>
    A cubic yard is 27 cubic feet, so it equals <strong>13.5 bags of 2 cubic feet</strong> or 9 bags of 3 cubic feet.
    Most garden centers sell 2 cubic foot bags. Once you need more than a yard or two, bulk mulch delivered by the yard
    usually costs less than bags.
  </p>

  <h2 id="coverage">Mulch coverage by depth</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Depth</th><th class="num">Sq ft per cubic yard</th><th class="num">Sq ft per 2 cu ft bag</th><th class="num">Sq ft per 3 cu ft bag</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {coverage.map((c) => <tr><td>{c.d} in</td><td class="num">{fmt(c.yd, 0)}</td><td class="num">{fmt(c.bag2, 0)}</td><td class="num">{fmt(c.bag3, 0)}</td></tr>)}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="depth">How deep should mulch be?</h2>
  <p>
    Two to three inches suits most flower and shrub beds: deep enough to hold moisture and block weeds, shallow enough for
    water and air to reach the soil. If last year's mulch is still there, add about an inch or two to bring it back to
    that depth. Keep mulch pulled a few inches back from tree trunks and plant stems, since mulch piled against bark holds
    moisture where you don't want it.
  </p>

  <p>
    Prefer something that won't break down? <a href="/landscape-rock-calculator/">Decorative rock</a> lasts for years. For a mix of materials across
    the yard, <a href="/cubic-yard-calculator/">convert each area to cubic yards</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <h3>How much does a yard of mulch weigh?</h3>
  <p>
    Roughly 400 to 800 pounds, depending on the wood and how wet it is. Mulch is sold by volume, so weight rarely matters
    except for hauling it in your own truck.
  </p>
  <h3>Why add extra?</h3>
  <p>
    Fresh mulch settles after the first few waterings and breaks down through the season. Ten percent extra keeps the
    finished depth where you planned it.
  </p>
  <h3>Does this work for wood chips, pine straw, or rubber mulch?</h3>
  <p>
    It works for anything sold by volume, including wood chips and rubber mulch. Pine straw is sold by the bale, and
    coverage depends on the bale size printed by the seller.
  </p>
  <p>
    Planting a new bed? Work in fresh soil first with the <a href="/topsoil-calculator/">topsoil calculator</a>, then
    mulch on top.
  </p>

  <h2 id="method">How this calculator works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Volume = area × depth, converted to cubic yards (27 cubic feet per yard).</li>
    <li>Bag counts assume 2 and 3 cubic foot bags and always round up.</li>
    <li>Bulk orders round up to the next half yard.</li>
  </ul>
</CalcPage>
